# Floating-point numbers as text, and text as floating-point numbers, exactly as `refined run`
# writes and reads them (D-125, D-129): the shortest digits that read back as the value, nearest
# it, laid out as Python's repr, and correctly rounded reading, both computed exactly with the
# bignum module (refined_write_float, refined_format_float, refined_parse_float).
#
# This module is generated from tools/floats.dsl by tools/build.py; its test cases come from
# tools/floatcases.py.

floats is module
floats purpose "Floating-point numbers as text and text as floating-point numbers, exactly as `refined run` writes and reads them (D-125, D-129)"
floats path floats
floats imports standard.machine
floats imports standard.string
floats imports standard.math
floats imports standard.compare
floats imports standard.convert
floats imports standard.bits
floats imports standard.logic
floats imports standard.test
floats imports numbers
floats imports output
floats imports regions
floats imports bignum
floats exports formatFloat
floats exports parseFloat

memoryAccess is capability
memoryAccess purpose "Authority to keep the numbers and digits of a conversion in machine.state and to write text by address"
memoryAccess grants use machine.memory none

systemCalls is capability
systemCalls purpose "Authority to write a number to standard output or standard error and to take a region's memory"
systemCalls grants use system.calls none

# machine.state bytes 40960 to 49151 belong to this module: five numbers of the bignum module (1,608 bytes each)
# from 40960, a float's digits at 49008, its text at 49040, and what reading text found at 49104 (how many
# significant digits) and 49112 (the bits of the result).
# Writing a Float64 is Python's repr, which `refined run` uses: the shortest digits that read back as the same
# value, the nearest of them to it, an exact tie going to the even digit (Steele and White's free-format method as
# Burger and Dybvig give it, computed exactly with bignums); laid out with an exponent (`1e+16`, `1.5e-07`) when
# the decimal point is 4 or more places left of the first digit or more than 16 right of it, and otherwise as
# digits with a point and at least one digit after it (`100.0`, `0.001`); `nan`, `inf`, and `-inf` for the rest.
# Reading text is correctly rounded, ties to even, as strtod and Python read it: the significand's digits (at
# most 800 kept, and one more standing for any that are dropped and not zero) over a power of ten, divided
# exactly to 54 bits and a remainder.
numbersAt is constant
numbersAt purpose "Where the five numbers start, in machine.state"
numbersAt type Int64
numbersAt value 40960

digitsAt is constant
digitsAt purpose "Where a float's digits are put, in machine.state"
digitsAt type Int64
digitsAt value 49008

textAt is constant
textAt purpose "Where a float's text is put together, in machine.state"
textAt type Int64
textAt value 49040

keptAt is constant
keptAt purpose "Where reading text leaves how many significant digits it kept, in machine.state"
keptAt type Int64
keptAt value 49104

bitsAt is constant
bitsAt purpose "Where reading text leaves the bits of the result, in machine.state"
bitsAt type Int64
bitsAt value 49112

numberBytes is constant
numberBytes purpose "The bytes of one of the bignum module's numbers"
numberBytes type Int64
numberBytes value 1608

mostKept is constant
mostKept purpose "How many significant digits of a text are kept exactly; more than any double's halfway point needs"
mostKept type Int64
mostKept value 800

